In C, one ends a program by returning from `main`, most commonly:

```c
int main(void) {
    /* your code */
    return 0;   /* success */
}
```

Or, from within a function, invoke `exit()` from `<stdlib.h>`:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
}
```

For failure:

```c
exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
```

`return` from `main` and `exit()` perform normal cleanup, including flushing standard I/O streams and calling functions registered with `atexit()`.

If you require immediate termination without normal cleanup—useful only in grim, exceptional circumstances—POSIX systems provide `_Exit()`:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

_Exit(1);
```

On Unix-like systems, `abort()` terminates abnormally and may produce a core dump:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

abort();
```
